# The app is rendered by the device WebView, whose version is tied to the
# device and not to us, so the build target has to be pinned deliberately.
#
# Why not the default: Angular otherwise emits class static initialization
# blocks (`static { ... }`), which need Chrome 94 (Sept 2021). The WebView on
# the Android 10 system image is Chrome 91, which cannot PARSE that -- the app
# is a blank white screen and the only clue is a single
# "Uncaught SyntaxError: Unexpected token '{'" in logcat.
#
# Why not lower: below roughly Chrome 89 the bundler stops emitting native ESM
# for lazy routes and falls back to `require()`, which no browser provides.
# That fails differently and just as fatally -- "Calling `require` for
# ./chunk-*.js in an environment that doesn't expose the `require` function",
# then "Constructor for ion-app was not found", and again a blank screen.
#
# 90 is the floor that clears both. Devices older than Android 10 are covered
# only insofar as their WebView has been updated through the Play Store, which
# is the normal case; a device pinned to its original WebView is not supported.
# src/polyfills-legacy.ts supplies the runtime built-ins this target lacks.
Chrome >= 90
